# Chapter 4, second part: formats on the edit screen and the Global menu
# with the keys that every panel has.  Part of the chapter source.

.h2 Formats on the edit screen [format-screen]
.ix Edit screen, formats
.ix Formats on the edit screen
.ix Screen, effect of formats
.ix Ruler
.ix Ruler line
.ix Expanded status line
The edit screen draws the text in character cells.  It shows the formats
that a character cell can show, as the layout of the text: attributes,
justification, indents, tab stops, section tags and column widths.  The
lines at the top of the screen are described in {x:view-status},
{x:view-expanded} and {x:view-ruler}; this section says what in them and in
the text depends on the format.

.h3 What the screen draws [format-screen-draws]
.ix Justification on screen
.ix Indent on screen
.ix Tab stops on screen
.ix Section tags on screen
.ix Indent per Level on screen
.ix Even justification on screen
- {b:Attributes.}  Bold, italics and underscore words are drawn with the
  display attributes of the terminal or window.
- {b:Justification Left and Even.}  The text starts at the left edge of the
  block and is not stretched; Even looks the same as Left.
- {b:Justification Right.}  Each row ends one cell before the right edge,
  which is kept for the end of line mark.
- {b:Justification Center.}  Each row is centered between the left block
  indent and the right edge.
- {b:Indent First, All but First, All.}  The first row of a line, the other
  rows, or both are indented by the first tab stop of the format, or by 5
  cells when it has none.
- {b:Tab stops.}  A tab character fills the cells up to the next stop of
  the block, counted from the left block indent.  A stop of type R, C, D,
  comma or N places the text that follows by its end, middle, decimal point
  or comma.  Without any stop a tab goes to the next multiple of 5.  Past
  the last stop a tab is one cell wide.
- {b:Left block indent.}  The text, the section tag and the end of line mark
  of the block are moved to the right by that many cells.
- {b:Section tag.}  The tag (1.1.6, or the Section Tag of the level) is drawn
  before the first row of a headline, in the attribute of the headline.
- {b:Indent per Level.}  A block of a section of level L is moved to the
  right by the sum of the Indent to Next Level of the levels above it (the
  Indent per Level where a level sets none); a level 1 block is not moved.
  The shift stops 24 cells short of the width of the screen.
- {b:Column widths.}  A column that has a width keeps it; the other columns
  share what is left.  The rows of the columns of a block go side by side.
The font of a block, the spacing, the paper, the margins and the page
layouts show only on the printed page.  The blank rows between blocks are the
same whatever the spacing settings are.

.h3 The status line and the expanded status line [format-screen-status]
.ix Status line, format
.ix Just: Indent: L:
.ix Headline in the expanded status line
The first row of the screen is the status line; it holds the name of the
document, the attribute at the cursor, the position and the number of the
section.  When Global, Options, Expanded Status Line? is Yes, a second row
appears under it with the format of the block that holds the cursor, as the
Editor looks it up ({n:format-order}):
.code
 Just: Even    Indent: None           L: 0   Headline
.end
Just is Left, Right, Center or Even; Indent is None, First, All but First
or All; L is the left block indent of the block; and the word Headline
appears when the block is the headline of a section.  The row follows the
cursor and changes as it moves from block to block.  In a table it shows the
format of the column that holds the cursor, except that L is the indent of the
block.

.pic pictures/format-expanded-status.png 5 Expanded status line and ruler.

.h3 The ruler [format-screen-ruler]
.ix Ruler line, glyphs
.ix Tab stop marks on the ruler
.ix Block indent mark on the ruler
The ruler is the row under the status line (and under the expanded status
line).  It is always shown.  It draws a row of small dots over the whole
width and puts marks on it for the block that holds the cursor, counted from
the left edge of that block's column.
.table 15 45
Mark | Meaning
Filled squares | The left block indent: one mark for each cell of the indent.
Right pointing triangle | A left tab stop (the indent plus its position).
Left pointing triangle | A right tab stop (type R).
Caret | A center tab stop (type C).
Period | A decimal tab stop (type D).
Comma | A comma tab stop (type comma).
Number sign | A number tab stop (type N).
.end
A block with no tab stops shows no tab marks, though tabs in it still go to the
next multiple of 5.  A block whose format changes (a local format with
{k:F6}, a different global ruler) changes the ruler at once.

.h3 Attribute keys [format-screen-attrkeys]
.ix Attribute keys
.ix Ctrl-B
.ix Alt-I
.ix Ctrl-U
.ix Ctrl-N
.ix Alt-F6
.ix Set Attributes menu
The attributes of the text can be changed while typing with these keys.
They make a local setting of the text itself ({n:format-local-text}).
.table 10 11 36
Key | Attribute | Effect
{k:Ctrl-B} | Bold | Switches bold on or off for the text typed next.
{k:Alt-I} | Italics | The same for italics.  {k:Ctrl-I} is {k:Tab}.
{k:Ctrl-U} | Underscore | The same for underscored words.
{k:Ctrl-N} | Normal | Switches off all the attributes of the text.
{k:Alt-F6} | Set Attributes | A menu of the four attributes.
.end
{k:Alt-F6} opens the list Set Attributes, and the choice acts like the
key of the same name.  {x:edit} describes how an attribute is
applied to a highlighted block of text.

.h2 The Global menu [format-global-menu]
.ix Global menu
.ix F12, Global
.ix Menu bar, Global
.ix MENU-GLOBAL
.pic pictures/format-global-menu.png 5 The Global menu bar.
The Global menu is the second entry of the {k:F12} menu bar (File, Global,
Edit, Print, Tools, Quit).  Press {k:F12}, move to Global with
{k:Right} and press {k:Return}; or press {k:F12} and {k:G}.  The menu is a
bar of six entries on the top row, with the word MENU-GLOBAL at its right end
and a line of explanation under it, as in the picture.
.table 10 15 32
Entry | Hint line | Opens
Format | Global section settings | Fourteen panels, {x:format-format}.
Page | Page layout settings | Seven layouts, {x:format-page}.
Typeface | Font assignments | The Typeface List, {x:format-typeface}.
Marker | Marker settings | Nineteen panels, {x:format-marker}.
Options | Other settings | One panel, {x:format-options}.
Named | Edit, Delete ... | Edit or Delete, {x:format-named}.
.end
The whole hint of Named is "Edit, Delete named block format settings".
In the bar, {k:Left} and {k:Right} move the highlight (and wrap round from
the last entry to the first), {k:Home} and {k:End} go to the first and to the
last entry, {k:Return}, {k:Ins} and {k:Down} choose, a letter chooses the entry
whose name starts with it (F, P, T, M, O or N) and at once runs it,
{k:Esc} goes back one level, and {k:F12} closes the menu.

.h3 Keys that every panel has [format-panel-keys]
.ix Panel keys
.ix Ins accepts, Esc cancels
.ix PgUp, PgDn in panels
.ix Choice field
.ix Text field
.ix Dimmed fields
.ix Information fields
.ix Panel ring
The panels of the Global menu are forms: a column of fields, each with a
label at the left and a value at the right.  The keys of a form are
described in {x:view-form-keys}; these are the ones that matter here.  A bad
value is refused with one of the messages of {x:format-messages}.
.table 13 47
Key | Effect
{k:Ins} | Accept: check and apply every panel of the group.
{k:Esc} | Cancel: nothing of any panel of the group is applied.
{k:PgUp} | The next panel of the group, wrapping from the last to the first.
{k:PgDn} | The previous panel of the group, wrapping from the first to the last.
{k:Down}, {k:Tab}, {k:Return} | The next field; there is no wrap from the last.
{k:Up}, {k:Shift-Tab} | The previous field.
{k:Left}, {k:Right} | Previous, next choice; or the cursor of a text field.
{k:Space} | On a choice field the next choice, wrapping round.
{k:F2}, {k:Alt-+} | On a choice field: the list of the choices.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The start and the end of a text field.
{k:Backspace}, {k:Del} | Delete in a text field.
.end
A choice field is drawn in square brackets, such as [Bold].  A text field
is drawn on a row of dots.  An information field is plain text without dots
or brackets: the cursor skips it and it cannot be changed.  When the cursor
arrives on a text field its text is highlighted; the first key that is typed
replaces all of it, and the cursor keys or {k:Home} and {k:End} keep it.

All the panels of one group (the fourteen of Global Format, the seven of Page,
the nineteen of Marker) are read before the group is accepted and are
applied together, so a change on the third panel is not lost by a visit to
the fifth.  A panel is applied only where it differs from what it showed when
it was opened, and the document is marked as changed (a star in front of its
name) only when a setting changes.  A group that was cancelled leaves the
document exactly as it was.

The Typeface, Options and Named panels have no neighbors and no paging; their
hint line reads "Ins accepts, Esc cancels".

.note In the panels of the Format and Page groups, {k:PgUp} goes forward and
  {k:PgDn} goes back.  In the Marker group the ring order is described in
  {x:format-marker-ring}.
